From 4657a0d51946ad755159883754de4abb0f7144b6 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Fri, 21 Aug 2026 11:34:05 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 1/3] Add @workspace/localization: shared te/ta/hi
 transcreation rules
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

A dependency-free package the web app, mobile app and API server all import,
so a transcreated line is judged the same way everywhere: the server builds
prompts and gates renders with it, the web app warns a writer as they type.

What it knows:

- Per-locale editorial policy. Urban speech in Telugu, Tamil and Hindi is
  heavily code-mixed, so English in the *read* is fine in all three. Written
  tolerance is not the same: Tamil has a live purism culture and Latin-script
  English inside written Tamil reads worse there than anywhere else, while
  Devanagari with transliterated loanwords is the standard register for Hindi
  advertising. The lint severity for stray Latin follows that split.

- Syllable estimation, because when a cut is locked the constraint on a
  translated line is duration, and duration tracks syllables rather than words
  (Telugu words are long) or characters (an Indic akshara is one or two code
  points). Includes Hindi schwa deletion applied right-to-left, so सब counts as
  one syllable and करना as two, and a final pulli consonant in Tamil counts as
  a coda rather than a syllable.

- Subtitle limits and validation against the Netflix timed-text figures, which
  all three languages share: 42 characters per line, 2 lines, 22 cps for adult
  content. Plus bottom-heavy wrapping and orphan-line detection.

- SRT and WebVTT, written without a byte-order mark — a BOM is the most common
  reason a player renders the first cue as garbage — and parsed tolerantly,
  since files arrive from every editor there is.

- Mechanical lint that catches in milliseconds what a native reviewer would
  otherwise catch three days later: English left in Latin letters inside an
  Indic line, textbook coinages nobody says (अनुप्रयोग, అనువర్తనం, செய்யுங்கள்),
  and a brand or interface term that got translated when it had to stay put.

That last rule is the one that costs installs: if the app's button says
"Continue", the Hindi voiceover has to say "Continue", or the viewer hunts for
a control that does not exist. BrandVoiceProfile.uiIsLocalized decides whether
those labels are enforced as untranslatable.

52 tests, no dependencies, no I/O.

