From 465a606bccbe21aa924ba2b2129488363be60dc2 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Fri, 21 Aug 2026 11:50:41 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 2/3] Add script localization: POST /ai/localize-script and a
 Languages tab
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Turns a timed English script into Telugu, Tamil or Hindi that fits the same
cut, and shows the writer everything needed to judge it before anyone books a
studio.

Server
------
`lib/localization/transcreate.ts` builds a prompt from the brand voice
invariants and the target locale's editorial policy, then measures what comes
back rather than trusting it. Every cue carries its own syllable budget into
the prompt, derived from its duration, because the picture is locked and
Telugu and Tamil need roughly 40% more syllables than English to say the same
thing. The model also returns a blind back-translation in the same pass — the
point is meaning drift, not accuracy, so it is told explicitly not to restore
the original English from memory.

Long scripts chunk at 25 cues per call, small enough that the model keeps every
budget in view. A cue the model fails to return comes back with empty text and
a blocking issue rather than being dropped silently: a missing line in a dub is
far worse than a flagged one, because nobody notices until the render.

The route reuses the existing text-gen client (so provider switching, failover
and cost tracking all apply) and the existing caption credit rail — one credit
per target language, reserved up front and refunded per language, so a Tamil
timeout cannot cost the user their Hindi track. New `videoLocalization` kill
switch, stacked under the Video Studio switch.

Synchronous rather than a job, matching caption generation: this is a handful
of chat completions. Rendering video in these languages is the long-running
half and belongs on the video job runner.

Web
---
A third AI Studio tab at /studio?tab=localize. Upload an SRT (real timings, the
path that matters) or paste a script with a runtime, which distributes cue
durations by syllable weight so a draft can be sanity-checked before the edit
locks. Results show, per line: the target text, syllables against budget, every
mechanical issue, and the back-translation side by side. SRT and VTT download
without a byte-order mark.

Nothing is presented as finished. What ruins a dub is not a bad word choice —
it is a line nobody checked against the picture.

12 server tests, 8 web tests. Typecheck, spec lint and codegen drift all clean.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
Claude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01Awuy8FQBmV2yU171ePKJKv
