From 58cee6037c535730b7bf24b6f08d39256fb8bb99 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Fri, 21 Aug 2026 12:04:01 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 3/3] Add dubbing foundations: ASR timestamps and Indic-safe
 ffmpeg rendering
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

The two pieces a locked-cut dub needs that the codebase did not have. Both are
additive; nothing existing changes behaviour.

ASR timestamps
--------------
Transcription returned text and nothing else, so there was no way to know where
a line sat in a video. All four providers can report spans and now do, behind
an opt-in `timestamps` flag — the voice-note dictation button that was the
original caller keeps getting a plain string with no extra latency or payload.

Groq and OpenAI switch to verbose_json, Deepgram adds utterances=true,
AssemblyAI groups the word timings it already returns. `segments.ts` normalises
all of it into one shape: ordered, non-empty millisecond spans. Overlaps are
trimmed rather than dropped, because a provider occasionally reports a segment
starting a few milliseconds before the previous one ended and losing the line
is worse than nudging it.

`secondsToMs` rejects null explicitly instead of leaning on `Number()`, which
turns null into 0 — that would have pinned a segment with a missing timing to
00:00 rather than discarding it. A test caught it.

ffmpeg rendering
----------------
`dub.ts`, with two decisions worth stating.

Subtitles go through libass (the `subtitles` filter), not drawtext. The
existing caption compositor uses drawtext, which renders through libfreetype
with no complex-script shaping: Telugu and Tamil conjuncts come out reordered,
Devanagari matras detach from the shirorekha. libass shapes through HarfBuzz.
For Latin the two are indistinguishable, which is exactly why the bug would
survive review.

Fonts are resolved by explicit family and then verified, because fontconfig
never fails — `fc-match ":lang=ta"` returns FreeSans on a box with Noto Sans
Tamil installed. Asking by name and checking what came back is the only way to
know a real face was used, so a missing font raises MissingIndicFontError
instead of rendering tofu. replit.nix gains fontconfig and the Noto families.

`planAudioFit` bends playback rate by at most 8% to fit a take into its slot
and reports anything beyond that as an overrun for a human to fix by cutting a
word. Past that threshold a listener hears "rushed" without being able to say
why, and a rushed dub is the loudest signal that a video was localized rather
than made. A take shorter than its slot is padded, never stretched — silence
reads better than a dragged line.

Tests encode real media rather than asserting on argument strings. Two bugs
that only a real encode could show: `-t` truncates but never pads, so a track
whose last take ended early came out short, and `-shortest` then cut the video
to match. Both fixed with apad. The Tamil case reads back peak luma off a
black fixture to prove libass drew glyphs at all.

40 tests. They skip cleanly where ffmpeg or the fonts are absent.

